        "\n",
        "## Why GPs\n",
        "\n",
        "The first example shows how we can use bayesian methods for spatial interpolation. The original idea by  [Danie G. Krige](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Danie_G._Krige), was to dig holes in the ground and measure the amount of gold in the soil with the goal to then extrapolate the amount of gold in the soil in other locations and then decide where to dig next.\n",
        "This method is called kriging and is widely used in geostatistics and spatial statistics. \n",
        "\n",
        "<!--\n",
        "::: {#fig-nonparametrics-gps_2 .column-margin  group=\"slides\" width=\"53mm\"}\n",
        "![why GP2](/images/c6/C6-SL02.png)\n",
        "\n",
        "rocket booster surrogate model [Gramacy,Lee 2008] [Gramacy 2020]\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "-->\n",
        "\n",
        "- Why Gaussian processes (GPs)?\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#exm-1-krigging}\n",
        "### Geospatial prediction for Oil and Gold deposits : Kriging for spatial interpolation\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "Y(\\mathcal{X}) | D_N &\\sim \\mathcal{N}_{n'}(\\mu(\\mathcal{X}), \\Sigma(\\mathcal{X})) \\\\\n",
        "\\text{mean } \\mu(\\mathcal{X}) &= \\Sigma(\\mathcal{X}, X_N)\\Sigma^{-1}_{N} Y_N \\\\\n",
        "\\text{variance } \\Sigma(\\mathcal{X}) &= \\Sigma(\\mathcal{X}, \\mathcal{X}) - \\Sigma(\\mathcal{X}, X_N)\\Sigma^{-1}_N\\Sigma(\\mathcal{X},X_N)\\Sigma(\\mathcal{X},X_N)^\\top\n",
        "\\end{aligned}\n",
        "$$ {#eq-kriging-prediction}\n",
        "\n",
        "where $Y_N$ are the observed values at locations $X_N$ and $\\Sigma(\\cdot, \\cdot)$ is the covariance function of the Gaussian process with dimensions $n' \\times n$\n",
        "\n",
        "This has some nice properties:\n",
        "- The describe the best (minimising MSPE) linear unbiased predictor (BLUP) of the value at $\\mathcal{X}$ given the data $D_N$.\n",
        "- The prediction is a Gaussian distribution with a mean and variance that can be computed in closed form.\n",
        "- The mean is a linear combination of the observed values, which means that the prediction is a weighted average of the observed values.\n",
        "- The variance is a measure of uncertainty in the prediction, which can be used to guide the next steps in the exploration process (e.g., where to dig next).\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#exm-2-ocean-currents}\n",
        "### Ocean current where the velocity vector field varies by space & time\n",
        "\n",
        "> Given sparse observations of buoy velocities, oceanographers are interested in reconstructing ocean currents away from the buoys and identifying divergences in a current vector field.\n",
        "\n",
        "This example is taken from [@berlinghieri2023gaussianprocesseshelmholtzfluid];\n",
        " which extended earlier work in [@gonccalves2019reconstruction] and [@lodise2020investigating]. The idea is simple enough but the math of fluid dynamics is pretty complicated to begin with and I was intrigued how bayesian nonparametric methods could be used to solve it.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::: {#fig-nonparametrics-gps_1 .column-margin  group=\"slides\" width=\"53mm\"}\n",
        "![wbuoy trajectories and ocen current](/images/c6/C6-SL01.png)\n",
        "Drifting buoy trajectories in the Gulf of Mexico superimposed on surface currents c.f. [Ryan, Özgökmen 2023] [@zewe2023betterway] \n",
        "::::\n",
        "\n",
        "- Scientists get sparse observations of the current from buoys \n",
        "- Goal: estimate the current\n",
        "\n",
        ":::: {.callout-note collapse=\"true\"}\n",
        "## The Helmholtz Decomposition\n",
        "\n",
        "The motion of a volume element of a fluid, such as the ocean, can be decomposed into a divergent velocity and a rotational velocity.\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\underbrace{\\mathbf{F}}_{\\text{ocean  flow}} = \\underbrace{\\nabla \\Phi}_{divergent\\  velocity} + \\underbrace{\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{\\Psi}}_{rotational\\  velocity}\n",
        "$$ {#eq-helmholtz-decomposition}\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "where:\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\nabla \\Phi = \n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix} \n",
        "  \\frac{\\partial \\Phi}{\\partial x} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial \\Phi}{\\partial y} \n",
        "\\end{bmatrix} \n",
        "\\text{and } \n",
        "\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{\\Psi} = \n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix} \n",
        "  \\frac{\\partial \\Psi_2}{\\partial x} \\\\ -\\frac{\\partial \\Psi_1}{\\partial y}  \n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}\n",
        "$$ {#eq-helmholtz-decomposition-components} \n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\delta := \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{F} = \\frac{\\partial F_x}{\\partial x} + \\frac{\\partial F_y}{\\partial y} = \\frac{\\partial^2 \\Phi}{\\partial x^2} + \\frac{\\partial^2 \\Phi}{\\partial y^2}\n",
        "$$ {#eq-divergence-in-helmholtz-decomposition}\n",
        "\n",
        "Note: I replaced the *pesky superscripts* with x and y. \n",
        "However the original notation may be simpler to generalize and more amenable to conversion into code. Also subscripts tend to be used to index data point in ML/ these papers \n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\zeta := \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{F} = \\frac{\\partial F_y}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial F_x}{\\partial y} = \\frac{\\partial^2 \\Psi}{\\partial y^2} + \\frac{\\partial^2 \\Psi}{\\partial x^2} \n",
        "$$ {#eq-curl-in-helmholtz-decomposition}\n",
        "\n",
        "Bayesian Approach and Gaussian Process Prior.\n",
        "\n",
        "In this paper the authors used a Bayesian approach to inferring $F$. \n",
        "They assumes a likelihood, for a noise model, relating the observed buoy velocities to the field F.\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "y_m = F(x_m) + \\epsilon_m, \\quad \\epsilon_m \\stackrel{\\text{ind}}{\\sim} \\mathcal{N}(0, \\sigma^2_{\\text{obs}} I_2), \\quad 1 \\le m \\le M.\n",
        "$$ {#eq-likelihood-helmholtz-decomposition}\n",
        "\n",
        "for some $\\sigma^2_{\\text{obs}} > 0$ and independent noise across observations.\n",
        "\n",
        "with standard spatiotemporal kernels (e.g., squared exponential kernels).\n",
        "::::\n",
        "\n",
        "**Challenges**\n",
        "\n",
        "Data is sparse, expensive to collect and is not on a grid.\n",
        "Currents are highly nonlinear but smooth in space-time.\n",
        "We want uncertainty quantification.  $\\frac{abc}{}$\n",
        "\n",
        "A common theme I noticed in BNP is that MCMC is great for complex simulations. Once real data is available it turns out to be hard to integrate into the model.  \n",
        "This is where the BNP models start to shine. They allow smart tweaks that are still tractable but allow more principled integration of the data into the model.\n",
        "\n",
        "The authors used simulation (think parameter recovery study) where they created datasets where the data (currents and divergence) are fully specified yet the GP still failed to connect currents when the buoys are sparse and that the recovered divergence.\n",
        "\n",
        "Actually this reminds me of a theorem in complex analysis where it is shown that one can reconstruct a holomorphic function from its values on a curve or even from a sequence of points that have a limit point in the domain. This seems to be a similar phenomenon where the function is smooth enough that we can reconstruct it from sparse data...\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: \n",
        "  - c.f. \n",
        "    - [Gramacy,Lee 2008]\n",
        "    - [Gramacy 2020]\n",
        "- Example 2: Surrogate model\n",
        "  - The lift force of a rocket booster varies as a function of \n",
        "    - speed at re-entry, \n",
        "    - angle of attack, and \n",
        "    - sideslip angle\n",
        "  - Scientists can run expensive simulations at chosen input settings\n",
        "  - Goal: estimate how lift varies as a function of input settings\n",
        "- Example 3: learn (& optimize) performance in machine learning as a function of tuning parameters\n",
        "   - c.f.  [Snoek et al 2012; 2015; Garnett 2023]\n",
        "- Motif: \n",
        "  - Sparse, noisy, costly but but smooth data.\n",
        "  - Output may have a nonlinear relationship to the inputs.\n",
        "  - Want uncertainty quantification.\n",
        "  - Low-dimensional inputs.\n",
        "- Bonus benefits: \n",
        "  - Ease of use (software, tuning) \n",
        "  - Supports optimization of outcome \n",
        "  - Predictions & uncertainties over derivatives & integrals \n",
        "  - Module in more-complex methods\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-important icon=false}\n",
        "\n",
        "#### ⚡ Overthinking parameters in Bayesian nonparametric models\n",
        "\n",
        "- Let's talk about the elephant in the room nonparametrics do have parameters!\n",
        "- The classical form of a distribution based on a few fixed number parameters is replaced in these by a notion similar to a euclidean line which can be arbitrary extended. This is the nature of the **nonparametric** parameter vector $\\theta$.\n",
        "\n",
        "- To make the analogy more precise and what the above and many examples illustrate is that we often are interested in some behavior $Y$ but as we get more and more data $X$, we will notice that there are new types of behavior that we did not observe earlier.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## More Examples:\n",
        "\n",
        "- new species are being daily\n",
        "- new friend set appear as social networks expand\n",
        "- new entities appear in images as the data set expands\n",
        "- new wikipedia articles are discovered as we click on wikilinks\n",
        "- new alleles are identified as we sequence more genomes\n",
        "\n",
        "## Roadmap\n",
        "\n",
        "- A Bayesian approach\n",
        "- What is a Gaussian process?\n",
        "  - Popular version using a squared exponential kernel\n",
        "- Gaussian process inference.\n",
        "  - Prediction & uncertainty quantification.\n",
        "- Goal:\n",
        "  - Learn the mechanism behind standard GPs to identify benefits and pitfalls\n",
        "\n",
        "## A Bayesian approach\n",
        "\n",
        "![posterior](/images/c6/C6-SL04.png){#fig-nonparametics-posterior .column-margin group=\"slides\" width=\"53mm\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "![joint](/images/c6/C6-SL05.png){#fig-nonparametics-joint .column-margin group=\"slides\" width=\"53mm\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "$$ \n",
        "\\underbrace{\n",
        "  \\mathbb{P}r(unknowns \\mid data) }_{\n",
        "    \\substack{\n",
        "      \\text{Given the data we've seen} \\\\ \n",
        "      \\text{what do we know about } \\\\ \n",
        "      \\text{the underlying function?} \n",
        "    }\n",
        "  }\n",
        "\\propto \n",
        "\\underbrace{\n",
        "  \\mathbb{P}r(data \\mid unknowns) \n",
        "  \\mathbb{P}r(unknowns)}_{  \n",
        "    \\substack{\n",
        "      \\text{A (statistical) model} \\\\\n",
        "      \\text{that can generate} \\\\\n",
        "      \\text{functions and data} \\\\\n",
        "      \\text{of interest}\n",
        "    }\n",
        "  }\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Multivariate Gaussian using locations {#sec-multivariate-gaussian-locations}\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "![](/images/c6/C6-SL06.png){#fig-nonparametrics-multivariate-gaussian-locations .column-margin group=\"slides\" width=\"53mm\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "- $M =2$ bivariate Gaussian $[y^{(1)} y^{(2)}] \\sim \\mathcal{N}(\\mathbf{\\mu}, \\mathbf{\\Sigma})$\n",
        "- with $\\mu = [0,0]^\\top$ and $k = \\sigma^2 \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & \\rho \\\\ \\rho & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ where $\\rho$ is the correlation between the two dimensions.\n",
        "- What if we let the correlation $\\rho$ depend on the input $x$?\n",
        "  - Let $\\rho(x)= \\rho(\\mid x^{(1)} - x^{(2)} \\mid)$\n",
        "  - Where the correlation goes to 1 as the x's get close\n",
        "  - And goes to 0 as the x's get far\n",
        "\n",
        "- Next: Use a Similar setup but an M-long Gaussian instead of just a bivariate\n",
        "  - We have M locations (need not be in order): $X^{(m)} \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$\n",
        "  - We generate $[y^{(1)}, \\ldots,y^{(m)}]^\\top \\sim \\mathcal{N}(\\mathbf{\\mu}, \\mathbf{K})$\n",
        "  - with\n",
        "    $\\mu=0_M$ and \n",
        "    $K$ such that $\\mathbf{K}_{ij} = \\sigma^2 \\rho\\mid (x^{(i)}, x^{(j)}\\mid)$\n",
        "  - Let's try $\\rho(\\Delta)=\\exp(-\\tfrac{1}{2} \\Delta^2)$\n",
        "      - $\\rho(0)=1$ , \n",
        "      - $\\lim_{\\Delta \\to \\infty} \\rho(\\Delta)=0$ and \n",
        "      - $\\rho(\\Delta)$ is monotonically decreasing\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
